# REVIEWER_LANES.md

> Every automated review lane this repository has ever had, in effect or not.
> A lane is a **lane, not an authority**: it advises, and the authority matrix
> decides. This file is the record of what advises us, what used to, and why.

---

## 1. Lanes at a glance

| Lane | State | Since | Verdict / exact disqualifier |
|---|---|---|---|
| <LANE_NAME> | in effect | <YYYY_MM_DD> | <WHAT_IT_IS_GOOD_AT> |
| <LANE_NAME> | cancelled | <YYYY_MM_DD> | <THE_EXACT_DISQUALIFIER> |
| <LANE_NAME> | declined | <YYYY_MM_DD> | <WHY_IT_WAS_NEVER_ADOPTED> |

States: **in effect** (running and read), **cancelled** (was running, stopped),
**declined** (evaluated, never adopted), **removed** (config gone, owner-side
teardown confirmed).

**A disqualifier is exact or it is not a disqualifier.** "Did not work out" is
a mood. "Skips draft pull requests, and this repo reviews on drafts" is a fact
the next session can re-check in one minute and either confirm or overturn.

---

## 2. Per-lane block

Repeat one of these per lane, including the dead ones.

### <LANE_NAME>

- **State:** <IN_EFFECT_OR_CANCELLED>, since <YYYY_MM_DD>
- **Triggers:** <WHICH_EVENTS_FIRE_IT> (every push? open and ready only?)
- **Scope:** <WHICH_PATHS_IT_REVIEWS>
- **Instruction files:** <WHICH_FILES_IT_LOADS> (registered in the budget file)
- **Meter shape:** <THROTTLE_OR_HARD_STOP> - a throttle reopens after a window,
  a hard stop does not reopen until the period rolls. The two demand opposite
  reactions, and a lane whose shape is unrecorded gets waited on when it will
  never answer.
- **Caps and measured ceilings:** <MEASURED_LIMIT_AND_HEADROOM> - measured, not
  quoted from documentation. A config field that grows by policy will cross a
  vendor cap, and past the cap the tool can reject the whole config and keep
  running **on its defaults**, looking entirely normal.
- **Standing rules for this lane:** <WAIT_OR_SKIP_THRESHOLD>,
  <DRAFT_BEHAVIOR>, <WHICH_COMMANDS_ARE_NEVER_INVOKED>
- **Owner actions owed:** <WHAT_ONLY_THE_OWNER_CAN_DO> - uninstalling an app,
  changing a dashboard-side setting, adjusting branch protection. A
  cancellation an agent cannot finish is **tracked here**, never assumed done.

---

## 3. Standing rules that apply to every lane

- **Verify before accepting OR declining.** Both directions are claims.
- **A metered lane that cannot answer is named in the report, never waited on.**
- **One writer per thread**; a finding is answered by its owner, with proof when
  declined.
- **A lane that skips the state your work sits in is silently disabled.** A
  draft-skipping lane on a repo that reviews drafts reports nothing and looks
  configured. Decide that per lane, deliberately, and pin the choice with a
  test in both directions - flipping it back should fail CI.

---

## 4. History - so a later session does not re-litigate this

Append-only. Every state change, with the date and the reason, including the
reversals.

| Date | Lane | Change | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| <YYYY_MM_DD> | <LANE_NAME> | <ADDED_OR_CANCELLED_OR_REVERSED> | <REASON> |

Two of these rows in opposite directions within a week is not embarrassing; it
is the record that stops a third round. **A cancelled lane's record stays so it
is not re-adopted by amnesia** - without the history, the next evaluation reads
the marketing page, adopts it, and rediscovers the same disqualifier at the
same cost.

---

## 5. Salvage - a dead lane's findings outlive it

**A cancelled scanner's real findings are worked, not discarded with it.** The
lane's verdict was about the lane: its metering, its scoping, its output
format. It was never a verdict on the defects it found.

| Finding class the dead lane covered | What covers it now |
|---|---|
| <FINDING_CLASS> | <REPLACEMENT_MECHANISM_OR_NOTHING_YET> |

A row whose right-hand side is "nothing yet" is honest and is a gap on the
backlog. A row quietly deleted along with the lane is how a whole class of
defect stops being looked for and nobody can name the day it happened.

---

## 6. Maintenance

Written by `/reviewer-integration`: `add <lane>` creates or updates the lane's
block, `remove <lane>` records the cancellation with its disqualifiers and the
owner actions owed, `baseline <lane>` reconciles the block against the live
config and reports drift. Hand edits are fine; the next `baseline` will
reconcile them.
